#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:62
msgid ""
"The recommended solver in PyBaMM is the ```pybamm.IDAKLUSolver`` <https://"
"docs.pybamm.org/en/stable/source/api/solvers/idaklu_solver.html>`__: a "
"direct wrapper around the Sundials IDAS solver, written and customised by "
"the PyBaMM team to work well with PyBaMM's models. It is the default solver "
"for all models and is recommended for essentially all use cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:64
msgid "Two experimental JAX-based solvers are also available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:66
msgid ""
"```pybamm.IDAKLUJax`` <https://docs.pybamm.org/en/stable/source/api/solvers/"
"idaklu_jax.html>`__ (*experimental*): a `JAX <https://jax.readthedocs.io/en/"
"latest/index.html>`__ wrapper around the ``IDAKLUSolver`` that allows it to "
"be used within a JAX model. Recommended for use on Linux or macOS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:67
msgid ""
"```pybamm.JaxSolver`` <https://docs.pybamm.org/en/stable/source/api/solvers/"
"jax_solver.html>`__ (*experimental*): a pure JAX solver that uses the "
"``jax`` library to solve the model equations. Recommended for use on Linux "
"or macOS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:70
msgid "Solver features"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:72
msgid ""
"**Speed**: The IDAKLU solver is fast both for solving models and for post-"
"processing output variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:73
msgid ""
"**Sensitivity analysis**: The IDAKLU solver can compute forward model "
"sensitivities with respect to input parameters, using the Sundials IDAS "
"solver."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:74
msgid ""
"**Adjoint sensitivity analysis**: Either adjoint sensitivity analysis or "
"reverse-mode auto differentiation is useful to compute sensitivities when "
"the number of input parameters is very large (e.g. ML models). Currently "
"only the pure JAX solver supports reverse-mode auto differentiation; adding "
"adjoint sensitivity analysis to the IDAKLU solver is a future development "
"goal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:75
msgid ""
"**Parallelism**: If a list of input parameters is passed to the solver, the "
"IDAKLU solver can solve the model in parallel for each parameter using "
"multiple threads via OpenMP (useful, e.g., for parameter sweeps). If enough "
"threads are available and the model is large enough, it can also parallelise "
"a single solve using OpenMP and the Sundials NVECTOR_OPENMP implementation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:76
msgid ""
"**GPU acceleration**: Currently only the pure JAX solver supports GPU "
"acceleration. There is some experimental support for GPU acceleration via a "
"new JAX-based backend for the IDAKLU solver, but this is still in "
"development."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:77
msgid ""
"**Events**: The IDAKLU solver supports events, which trigger an action when "
"a certain condition is met (the main use case being to stop the solver "
"between steps of an experiment). It uses the underlying Sundials event "
"handling, which is fast and robust. The pure JAX solver does not support "
"events."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:78
msgid ""
"**Evaluation and interpolation points**: All solvers take a list of time "
"points to evaluate the solution (the ``t_eval`` argument), which stops the "
"solver at each requested point. The IDAKLU solver can additionally take a "
"list of interpolation points (the ``t_interp`` argument), interpolating the "
"solution at these points without stopping the solver, speeding up the "
"calculation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:79
msgid ""
"**Other features**: The Sundials solvers have many features that change how "
"the solver works (tolerances, linear solvers, minimum step sizes, etc.); see "
"the `Sundials docs <https://sundials.readthedocs.io/en/latest/>`__. Many of "
"these are exposed and documented in the IDAKLU solver (see the `docs "
"<https://docs.pybamm.org/en/stable/source/api/solvers/idaklu_solver."
"html>`__). If PyBaMM does not expose a feature you need, please post an "
"issue on the `GitHub page <https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM/issues>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:83
msgid "Solver timing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:85
msgid ""
"Below we look at the solve time for the ``IDAKLUSolver`` on the SPM, SPMe "
"and DFN models with their default options, separating the first solve (which "
"includes solver setup) from subsequent solves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"first_solve_time = np.zeros(3)\n"
"second_solve_time = np.zeros(3)\n"
"model_classes = [\n"
"    pybamm.lithium_ion.SPM,\n"
"    pybamm.lithium_ion.SPMe,\n"
"    pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN,\n"
"]\n"
"for i, model_cls in enumerate(model_classes):\n"
"    sim = pybamm.Simulation(model_cls(), solver=pybamm.IDAKLUSolver())\n"
"    start_time = time.perf_counter()\n"
"    sol = sim.solve([0, 3600])\n"
"    voltage = sol[\"Voltage [V]\"](0)\n"
"    first_solve_time[i] = time.perf_counter() - start_time\n"
"    start_time = time.perf_counter()\n"
"    sol = sim.solve([0, 3600])\n"
"    voltage = sol[\"Voltage [V]\"](0)\n"
"    second_solve_time[i] = time.perf_counter() - start_time\n"
"\n"
"labels = [m.__name__ for m in model_classes]\n"
"x = np.arange(3)\n"
"width = 0.35\n"
"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 5))\n"
"ax.bar(x - width / 2, first_solve_time, width, label=\"First solve (incl. "
"setup)\")\n"
"ax.bar(x + width / 2, second_solve_time, width, label=\"Second solve\")\n"
"ax.set_xticks(x)\n"
"ax.set_xticklabels(labels)\n"
"ax.set_ylabel(\"Time (s)\")\n"
"ax.set_title(\"IDAKLUSolver solve time\")\n"
"ax.legend()\n"
"plt.tight_layout()\n"
"plt.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:144
msgid ""
"The \"First solve\" bars include the solver setup time, which for these "
"relatively small problems is significant. The \"Second solve\" bars exclude "
"this setup time and give a clearer indication of the solver's speed for (a) "
"calculating a solution and (b) post-processing the solution to extract the "
"voltage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/03-pybamm-solvers.ipynb:146
msgid ""
"The IDAKLU solver is customised to work well with PyBaMM's models, and its "
"online (via ``t_eval``) and post-processing interpolation features mean "
"obtaining output variables is fast."
msgstr ""
